export const DE_C1_S01_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-gruess-gott-herr-krawczyk": [
    `When I moved from Berlin-Neukölln to Unterweilach in the Swabian Alb a year ago, I had prepared myself for a lot of things: for the cleaning rota, for Maultaschen, and for the fact that the last bus leaves at 6.40 p.m. and on Sundays there isn't one at all. What I wasn't prepared for was the greeting. In Berlin I had lived for twelve years in a building with forty households and had, at a rough guess, known three neighbours by name. You nodded to each other on the stairs when it couldn't be avoided, and regarded every further word as an intrusion.`,
    `In Unterweilach people greet each other. You greet the baker, the postwoman and the tractor driver coming towards you on the farm track, and, as I learned in the first week, also the people you don't know at all, in fact those above all. "Grüß Gott," said an old woman with a walking frame as I went to the baker's on my first morning. I mumbled something that, with a great deal of goodwill, could pass as "Morning." She stopped and stared after me as if I had stolen her walking frame.`,
    `By the evening the whole village knew that the new man from Berlin didn't say hello. I heard it from my landlady, Frau Haug, who explained the rules to me with the kind of patience otherwise reserved for children and dogs. Anyone you meet gets greeted. Whoever greets first has won. And anyone who doesn't greet back, "well, he's just a Berliner." She said it without the slightest malice, the way you'd remark that it's raining.`,
    `I decided to adapt and, as is my way, went about it thoroughly. I greeted everything. I greeted cyclists, joggers, drivers behind tinted windows, a herd of cows and, on a foggy November morning, a scarecrow. I greeted so eagerly that people began to whisper about whether something was wrong with the new man. Greeting, I learned, is not a courtesy in the Alb but a science. You don't greet too loudly or too often, and if you meet the same person twice in one day, the second time you don't say "Grüß Gott" again but "So": a single word that simultaneously means "Here we are again", "Nice weather today" and "I've got my eye on you".`,
    `By spring I had more or less got the hang of it. I said "Grüß Gott" at the right volume and "So" at the right moment, and on good days the baker even said "Ah, Herr Krawczyk," which in Unterweilach amounts roughly to honorary citizenship. Without noticing, I had become a little proud.`,
    `Then in May I went back to Berlin for a weekend. I got off the underground at Hermannplatz, the sun was shining, and a young man with headphones came towards me. "Grüß Gott," I said, loudly, clearly and with a friendly nod. He took off one earphone, looked me up and down and asked whether I wanted to sell him something. Then he said, "You all right, mate?" and walked on without waiting for an answer.`,
    `I stood at Hermannplatz for a while and felt like a tourist in my own past. I had lived here for twelve years without ever noticing how much effort it takes not to see one another. On Sunday evening I was back on the train heading south, and when I went to the baker's on Monday morning, the old woman with the walking frame came towards me. I had met her almost every day for a year and still didn't know her name.`,
    `"Grüß Gott," I said. "Grüß Gott," she said. Then she stopped. "You were away." It wasn't a question. "In Berlin," I said. She nodded slowly, as if I had told her about a serious illness I had survived. "Well, now you're back again," she said, and after a pause that was exactly as long as it had to be: "So."`,
  ],
  "de-die-stadt-die-es-nicht-gibt": [
    `For twenty-three years Anke Mewes had worked for Bielefeld's city marketing company, and for twenty-three years she had been answering letters from people informing her that she didn't exist. More precisely: that the city she worked in didn't exist. The claim was older than the internet, a student joke that had spread like a rumour and that no official denial could put to rest; quite the opposite. Every denial, as Anke knew by now, counted as further proof for the theory's followers. After all, who with nothing to hide would insist so stubbornly that they exist?`,
    `Most of the letters were meant as a joke, and Anke answered them with a standard reply and a ballpoint pen bearing the city's coat of arms. The letter from Eberhard Kolb of Rosenheim was different. It ran to eleven pages, contained three tables and a map on which Herr Kolb had marked journey times in red felt-tip, and it was entirely serious. He was, he wrote, a retired physics teacher and had all his life relied only on what he had measured himself. He had never met anyone who had been to Bielefeld. He therefore politely requested empirical proof.`,
    `Anke could simply have sent him the pen. Instead, on a whim she later couldn't explain even to herself, she wrote back: the best proof would be a visit. She was inviting him. Two weeks later the answer came, this time on a postcard. He accepted the invitation and would arrive on 14 June at 11.52 on the ICE from Hanover. He did, however, ask for her understanding that he reserved the right to draw his conclusions only once the investigation was complete.`,
    `Herr Kolb was a small, precise man with a sun hat and a notebook in which he wrote everything down. Anke showed him the Sparrenburg castle, from which you could see the whole city. He noted: "Panorama. Plausible, but not conclusive." She showed him the university, whose main building was so big that even students got lost in it. He noted: "Impressive. Could be a stage set." She took him into the old town and bought him a Pickert, a kind of potato pancake with raisins that was only eaten in this region, and he chewed on it for a long time, thoughtfully. "That," he said at last, "nobody would make up."`,
    `In the afternoon, on a bench in Siegfriedplatz, he grew quieter. He leafed through his notebook without writing anything, and then he took a photo out of his inside jacket pocket. It showed a young woman with short hair in front of a house with a green door. "My wife," he said. "She was born here. At eighteen she left and never came back. When anyone asked her where she was from, she said: from Bielefeld, but that doesn't exist, does it. She found that funny. I think she meant something else." He was silent for a while. "She died in the winter. I didn't know how to travel here without having a reason. So I went and found one."`,
    `Anke looked at the photo for a long time. Then she asked whether he knew the address. He did. The house was still standing, two streets away. The door had been painted grey since then, and in the window hung the sign of a physiotherapy practice. Herr Kolb stood in front of it for a while, hands behind his back, like someone watching an experiment whose result he has long known. Then he photographed the door, once, carefully, and put his phone away.`,
    `At the station he shook her hand. "I regret to inform you," he said, "that my investigation has not reached a definitive result. I have seen a place in which I found myself. Science cannot claim more than that." He paused. "But my wife was wrong. It exists. It very much exists." Then he got on the train to Hanover, punctually at 17.04.`,
    `On Monday Anke put her standard reply to one side. In the city marketing office's visitors' book, where school classes usually wrote their names, she entered a single sentence: "Existence confirmed on 14 June by a physicist from Rosenheim, with reservations." A week later a postcard arrived without any text. On the front was a picture of the Sparrenburg. It had been postmarked in Bielefeld.`,
  ],
  "de-die-brunnenfrage": [
    `For nineteen years Regula Zellweger had been the municipal clerk of Hinterwies, a village in the Appenzell region with 812 inhabitants, eleven farms, a cheese dairy and a fountain. The fountain stood in the middle of the village square, a bulky trough of grey sandstone on which the year 1871 could be read in weathered figures. Since last winter a crack had run right across the trough, and on dry days it lost more water than it held. That something had to be done, everyone agreed. What they disagreed about had divided the village more thoroughly over the past six months than any inheritance dispute.`,
    `The municipal council had proposed replacing the old trough. A young artist from Zurich had supplied a design: a slender basin of brushed stainless steel over which the water flowed in a thin, shining veil. Cost: 180,000 francs, half of it paid by the canton. The opponents, led by the retired teacher Ernst Fässler, spoke of a "Zurich spaceship" and demanded that the old fountain be restored, whatever the cost. Letters to the editor were written, friendships ended, and in the cheese dairy one farmer is said to have stopped selling cheese to another because of the fountain question.`,
    `One Friday evening in March the item finally came before the municipal assembly. The gym was as full as it had last been for the vote on the bypass road. The mayor read out the motion, Fässler gave a speech in which the words "homeland" and "respect" each came up eleven times, and the artist, who had travelled specially from Zurich, showed pictures of her design that set off a murmur in the hall which, with a great deal of goodwill, could be interpreted as interest.`,
    `Then they voted, as people have always voted in Hinterwies: by a show of hands. Regula counted. She counted a second time and, because the result stayed the same, a third. Sixty-three for the new fountain, sixty-three against. The hall became so quiet that you could hear the hum of the strip lights. A tie. In nineteen years she had never seen that. According to the rules, the mayor would now have had to cast the deciding vote, and Regula could see from his face that he would rather have walked barefoot over the Säntis. However he decided, he wouldn't be drinking coffee with the other half of the village even in twenty years' time.`,
    `At that moment the door of the gym opened. In came Frau Anna Bänziger, pushed in her wheelchair by her great-grandson; at ninety-three she was the oldest voter in the municipality. She was wearing her Sunday costume and an expression that tolerated no contradiction. "I'm late, I know," she said. "The bus. Has the vote already happened?" The mayor looked to Regula for help. Regula looked in the rulebook. A vote, it said, was only considered closed once the chairman had announced the result. He hadn't announced anything yet.`,
    `A murmur went through the hall, and everyone knew what everyone was thinking. Anna Bänziger had lived on the village square all her life. As a child she had drunk from this fountain, as a young woman she had rinsed the washing in it, and it was said that her late husband had proposed to her at its edge. Fässler was already smiling. He was so sure of himself that he had half stood up to shake her hand.`,
    `Frau Bänziger had them show her the pictures of the design, for a long time and in silence. Then she raised her hand. "For the new one," she said. Fässler sat down again, very slowly. "Frau Bänziger," he said, "it's your fountain, surely. Your husband proposed to you there..." "For eighty years I've looked at that fountain," she interrupted him. "I know every stone. My husband did propose to me there, that's true, and he fell into the water doing it, only nobody tells that part. Before I die I'd like to see something new on this square once more. Something I don't already know by heart."`,
    `Regula entered the result, sixty-four to sixty-three, in the minutes in her neatest handwriting. The old trough, the council decided a week later, to save the peace at least, was not taken to the rubbish dump but set up in front of the old people's home, where it has served as a flower bed ever since. Frau Bänziger, who lives there, waters it every morning. She claims she only does it because nobody else would think of it.`,
  ],
  "de-beileid-automatisch": [
    `The company was called Trostwerk, had its offices on the fifth floor of a glass building in Düsseldorf's Media Harbour, and sold something Lea Brandt didn't like explaining at parties: letters of condolence written by an artificial intelligence. You entered the name of the deceased, your relationship to the bereaved and two or three memories, and after eleven seconds there was a card in front of you that sounded as if you had struggled over every word for a whole night. The product was called "Mitgefühl Pro". Lea, who used to write advertising copy for skin cream, was responsible for the tone.`,
    `She had made her most important discovery in the first month. She had fed the system with thousands of real letters of condolence, donated by undertakers, archives and people who didn't know what to do with their shoeboxes full of post. The letters that the recipients had described as particularly comforting were not the most beautiful ones. They were the clumsiest. Sentences that broke off. Repetitions. A wrong comma, a crossed-out word. Anyone who phrased things too smoothly, Lea concluded, seemed to have death already under control, and that was exactly what people couldn't forgive.`,
    `So she taught the machine to be awkward. The module was called "Stumble" internally, and it was her pride and joy. It scattered a small typo into every third card, left a sentence unfinished now and then, and at the statistically optimal point inserted an "I don't even know what to write." Customer satisfaction rose by forty per cent. At the Christmas party the managing director, Fabian Lorenz, a man who considered ties a sign of insecurity, made a speech in her honour. "Lea has taught the machine to hesitate," he said. "That's more than most people can do." Everyone applauded.`,
    `In February Lea's father died at seventy-one of a heart attack, while shovelling snow in front of his terraced house in Wuppertal. She drove there, sorted out what had to be sorted out, and in the evening sat in his kitchen, where the newspaper from the day he died still lay open at the crossword. He hadn't found a six-letter word for "grief". Or he hadn't got round to it.`,
    `The cards came in the days that followed. One of them was sent by Fabian Lorenz on behalf of the whole team, and it was wonderful. It called Lea's father by his first name, it struck the right balance between warmth and restraint, and in the third sentence it said: "I don't even know what to write." In the fifth it said "sincreely". Lea read the card twice. Then she laid it face down on the kitchen table, as carefully as if it might break.`,
    `The last card was in the letterbox on Saturday, without a stamp. It was from Herr Yılmaz across the road, a widower with whom her father had argued about football over the garden fence for thirty years. The handwriting was shaky, the paper squared, obviously torn out of a maths exercise book. It had exactly two sentences on it: "Your father put my bin out on the street every Tuesday because I can't manage it any more with my back. Who's going to do it now." No question mark. No greeting. A stain that might have been coffee.`,
    `Lea sat for a long time with that squared slip of paper in her hand, and for the first time since the call from the hospital she cried. It wasn't a beautiful letter. It wasn't even a letter of condolence in the proper sense. It was the only thing anyone had written just for her that week without thinking about the effect it would have.`,
    `On Monday she rang Düsseldorf and took another week off. On Tuesday morning at half past six, while it was still dark and had snowed again, she crossed the road, dragged Herr Yılmaz's grey bin through the snow to the kerb and placed it exactly the way her father always had: handle towards the street, lid shut. Across the road a light came on behind a curtain. She raised her hand, and after a moment a hand was raised there too.`,
  ],
  "de-zwei-tassen-auf-einmal": [
    `In the autumn of 1923, a cup of coffee in Berlin cost something different in the morning than in the afternoon, and Grete Wolff, a waitress at the Café Brandes in Schöneberg, had learned to write the prices on a board in chalk because they had to be wiped off twice a day. In the summer a cup had still cost a few thousand marks, in October it was millions, and one Monday in November Grete wrote a word on the board for the first time that until then she had only known from arithmetic lessons: billions.`,
    `The customers came anyway, or precisely because of it. Those who had money spent it before it was worth nothing, and those who had none at least sat somewhere warm. Workers were now paid twice a day, and at midday women stood at the factory gates, took the pay packets out of their husbands' hands and ran straight to the baker's. Herr Brandes, a fat man with nervous hands, spent his days counting banknotes, which he carried down to the cellar in laundry baskets every evening. People told the story of a man who had had such a basket stolen: the thieves had tipped the money out onto the street and taken only the basket.`,
    `One customer particularly caught Grete's eye. Herr Morgenroth, a piano teacher with a threadbare coat and surprisingly clean shoes, came every afternoon at three and always ordered two cups of coffee at once. The first time, she had asked whether he was expecting someone. "Only the future, Fräulein," he had said. "And it's getting more expensive." He drank the first cup hot and the second cold, because the second, ordered at four, would already have cost twice as much. Herr Brandes considered it fraud. Grete considered it the only sensible calculation she had seen in months.`,
    `One wet Thursday Morgenroth came later than usual. He ordered his two cups, drank them in silence and then put a wad of notes on the table, which, he said, the mother of one of his pupils had paid him with that morning. Grete counted. In the morning the money would have been enough for ten cups. Since then the price on the board had been wiped off twice. It wasn't enough for even one. Morgenroth looked at the notes as if they had insulted him personally. Then he stood up and said very calmly that his coat would pay the rest. He began to take it off.`,
    `"Keep it on," said Grete. She glanced at the counter, where Herr Brandes had his back to her, bundling notes, and pushed the money into her apron pocket. "That's fine." Morgenroth looked at her for a long time. "Fräulein Wolff," he said, "you're a bad liar. But I accept, on one condition. You once told me that as a child you wanted to play the piano. Come to my place on Sunday, Goltzstraße 14, third floor. The lesson costs two cups of coffee. That's the only price in this city that isn't going up."`,
    `She went. She went every Sunday throughout November and, on an out-of-tune grand piano whose ivory keys had turned yellow, learned the first bars of a sonata whose name she couldn't remember. Morgenroth was a strict teacher and just as bad a liar as she was. He claimed she had talent.`,
    `In mid-November the Rentenmark arrived. Overnight, it seemed, money had weight again. Herr Brandes wiped the board and wrote a price on it which, to Grete's astonishment, stayed there for a whole week. The laundry baskets disappeared from the cellar. People ordered like human beings again, one cup after another, and drank it while it was hot.`,
    `Morgenroth came back too, at three on the dot, in his threadbare coat. Grete was already standing next to him with her pad. "One cup, Herr Morgenroth? It's not worth ordering two any more." He nodded. "One cup," he said. Then, after a brief hesitation, as if he had to run through the sum once more: "No. Two. And if Herr Brandes has no objection, why don't you sit with me while the second one is still hot."`,
  ],
};

